From f3857ad7a468c3bf5d54336ccf7769701d6a5a45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joeri Van Dooren <ure@on6ure.be>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] app: P2P listening only while the radio's APP > Mail has
 Listen on (OpenX6X00)

The radio may transmit only with someone at it. Pat took its P2P
listening from the config's listen (the Configure page's "Listen for
inbound P2P traffic") and --listen, at each start and at each reload,
which that page's Save makes: ticked, the radio answered any station
that called it, with nobody there, on whatever frequency it was on. The
supervisor wrote listen [] at each start of Pat, which a Save and its
reload undid until the next one.

With OPENX6X00_LISTEN_GATE set (the supervisor, x6100-winlink, sets it
to /run/winlink/listen), neither is used. Pat listens on the transport
that file names, ardop or varahf, while the file is fresh, and on
nothing else: the radio's GUI writes it each second while its APP >
Mail is open with Listen on, stamped with the time since boot (the
wall clock may jump when the radio sets its clock), and removes it
when Listen goes off, the app is closed or another app opens, the
radio leaves the frequency, and as it starts. A file not stamped for
3 s is from a GUI that is gone or hangs. Pat looks at it twice a
second: a listener turned on another way (the interactive mode's
listen) goes too. Each change is a line in Pat's log, which the
mailbox's log pane shows. Unset, as anywhere else, Pat listens as
upstream.

A listener that goes sends the modem LISTEN FALSE (ARDOP) or LISTEN OFF
(VARA HF), as upstream's; a session that came in before goes on to its
end.

The Configure page's "Listen for inbound P2P traffic" under ARDOP and
VARA HF says it is not used on the radio, and where listening is.

With tests (app/listen_gate_test.go: the file, the clock, the
listeners kept to it).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
